  "data": {
    "text": "Brain flexibility associated with need for cognition contributes to creative achievement\n\nPsychophysiology. 2019;56:e13464. https://doi.org/10.1111/psyp.13464\n\nAll functional imaging data are freely available for re-\nsearch from the International Neuroimaging Data‐Sharing \nInitiative (http://dx.doi.org/10.15387/ fcp_indi.retro.slim). \nSpecifically, 164 participants (70 male) with both imaging \ndata and NFC scores were included in this study, and some \nof them (N = 100) also completed the Creative Achievement \nQuestionnaire (CAQ). All participants were Han, aged from \n17 to 26 years old, the mean was 19.99 ± 1.12. We used two \nexclusion criteria to ensure imaging data quality: (a) three \nparticipants were excluded whose maximal head motion \nbetween volumes in any direction was > 3 mm or rotation \nin any axis > 3° during scanning, (b) all participants exhib-\nited relatively few bad volumes (defined as any volume with \nframewise displacement (FD) value > 0.5 mm) that ranged \nfrom 0 to 9.91%; thus, no one was excluded according to the \nwidely used criterion based on > 10% bad volumes. Through \nself‐report questionnaires and structured and semistructured \ninterviews, none had a history of psychiatric illness, cogni-\ntive disorders, substance abuse, or MRI contraindications. \nThe study was approved by the Research Ethics Committee \nof the Brain Imaging Center of Southwest University, and \nwritten informed consent was obtained from each participant.\n\nAll imaging data were collected on a Siemens 3T Trio scanner \n(Siemens Medical Systems, Erlangen, Germany). Resting-\nstate fMRI data were obtained with gradient echo‐planar \nimaging (EPI) sequences: repetition time (TR) = 2,000 ms, \necho time (TE) = 30 ms, field of view (FOV) = 220 × 220, \nflip angle = 90°, slices = 32, thickness = 3.0 mm, slice \ngap = 1.0 mm, voxel size = 3.4 × 3.4 × 4 mm3. This scanning \nlasted 8 min and contained 242 volumes; all participants were \ninstructed to close their eyes and rest but not to fall asleep.\n\nThe fMRI data were preprocessed using the advanced \nData Processing Assistant for Resting‐State fMRI (DPARSF, \nhttp://www.rfmri.org/DPARSF; Yan, Wang, Zuo, & Zang, \n2016; Yan & Zang, 2010), which is based on Statistical \nParametric Mapping (SPM, http://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/\nspm). The first 10 volumes were discarded to suppress the \nequilibration effects and consider subjects’ adaptation to \nthe environment. The remaining volumes were slice‐timing \ncorrected, realigned, and spatially normalized to the MNI \n(Montreal Neurological Institute) template and resampled to \n3 × 3 × 3 mm3. Then, we conducted a series of noise clean-\nup strategies, including wavelet de‐spiking, nuisance signal \nregression, and data scrubbing. Specifically, the performance \nof wavelet de‐spiking has been shown to be superior to the \nstandard de‐spiking algorithms, and the default parameters \nwere used, which were equivalent to prior studies (Patel et al., \n2014). Nuisance signals including white matter, cerebrospi-\nnal fluid, and head motion parameters and their derivatives \nusing the Friston 24‐parameter model were regressed out \nto control the potential influence of physiological artifacts \n(Friston, Williams, Howard, Frackowiak, & Turner, 1996). \nWe further implemented data scrubbing to better address \nhead motion concerns. The bad time points were regarded as \nregressors that defined as volumes with FD Power > 0.5 mm \nas well as the two succeeding volumes and one preceding \nvolume to reduce the spillover effect of head motion (Power, \nBarnes, Snyder, Schlaggar, & Petersen, 2012). In addition, \nlinear and quadratic trends were also included as regressors \nsince the BOLD signal exhibits low‐frequency drifts. We \nnext conducted spatial smoothing with 4‐mm full‐width at \nhalf‐maximum Gaussian kernel and band‐pass temporal fil-\ntering (0.01–0.1 Hz). Finally, given that no consensus has \nbeen reached on global signal regression (GSR) in fMRI \nstudies (Caballero‐Gaudes & Reynolds, 2017; Liu, Nalci, & \nFalahpour, 2017), we performed preprocessing with and \nwithout GSR, and the results of GSR are represented in the \nonline supporting information, Appendix S1.\n\nA graph is made up of nodes and edges in graph theory \nanalysis. For node definition, we employed a 264‐node \nfunctional atlas that is widely distributed across the whole \nbrain and includes 13 large‐scale brain networks (Power \net al., 2011), as shown in Figure 1a. ... The time series of each node was acquired by \naveraging the BOLD signals from all voxels in this region, \nand the Pearson correlation of the time series between pairs \nof nodes was calculated to represent the edges (i.e., FC). \nA sliding window approach was used to construct a dy-\nnamic FC matrix for each participant. ... the \nwindow length was therefore set to 50 TRs and the time \ngap of each window was set to 1 TR, yielding 183 overlap-\nping time windows ... and then Fisher's z transformation \nwas conducted to improve normality.\n\nTo explore the spatiotemporal segregation of nodes in dy-\nnamic FC, we connected the adjacent time windows by \nadding interlayer connections (temporal link) between each \nnode and itself in the adjacent window (Bassett et al., 2011; \nMucha et al., 2010), as shown in Figure 1b. Then, a gen-\neralized Louvain algorithm was used to find the putative \nfunctional modules based on the optimization of the mod-\nularity quality function (Mucha et al., 2010). ... We therefore performed the multilayer community detection \nalgorithm 100 times to yield optimized partitions of the mul-\ntilayer modularity for each time (Bassett et al., 2011, 2013).\n"
  },
